    "--- \n",
    "## 3. Memory References & Object Mutability\n",
    "\n",
    "In Python, variables do not hold values directly; they store **references** (memory addresses) to objects allocated on the heap. \n",
    "- **Immutable Objects:** `int`, `float`, `str`, `tuple`, `frozenset` (Cannot be modified after creation).\n",
    "- **Mutable Objects:** `list`, `dict`, `set`, custom classes (Can be modified in-place)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": None,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Demonstrating Variable Assignment (Reference Binding)\n",
    "a = [1, 2, 3]\n",
    "b = a  # Both 'a' and 'b' point to the SAME memory address\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Address of a: {hex(id(a))}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Address of b: {hex(id(b))}\")\n",
    "print(f\"a is b: {a is b}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# In-place mutation affects both variable bindings\n",
    "b.append(99)\n",
    "print(f\"Updated a: {a}\")"
   ]
  },
